  "image_name": "2d7e2c211da2704d25bba5dbd3f63930.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how a neuromorphic system can implement neuron and synapse functions using circuit elements, likely involving FeFET devices. On the left, the neuron is shown as a leaky integrate-and-fire unit: input current charges a capacitor, the membrane voltage rises over time, and once it crosses a threshold it generates a spike sent to the synapse before resetting. The accompanying circuit sketch highlights the resistor-capacitor dynamics and threshold behavior that produce this pulse-like output.\n\nOn the right, the synapse is depicted as an array of programmable weight cells organized by word lines, bit lines, and selection lines. This structure supports cell programming and multiply-accumulate computation. Two operating modes are emphasized: a current-domain mode, where output current is the sum of weighted voltages, and a time-domain mode, where delay or pulse width accumulates with input-weight contributions. The array therefore acts as a memory-compute block for synaptic weighting.\n\nThe bottom row connects these concepts to hardware realizations. The neuron appears to combine excitatory and inhibitory inputs, while the synapse is implemented as a crossbar-like weight cell array and a time-domain output circuit. Overall, the figure links abstract neural functions to practical FeFET-based neuromorphic circuitry.",
